ef 17592186106043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186262676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105856 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1045 "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106019 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "91efd670-1647-54ae-8540-d95ee4791ec7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6da7c1a3-2c67-58ef-8929-9aec32acf858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106040 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "591204f9-fa5e-50e9-8549-d7f078fe4715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106043 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106822 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106822 #edb/ref 1061 35824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "76d73a7c-6865-5f65-9779-6beec167812a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106823 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106823 #edb/ref 1061 35838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c455c39-68b2-555e-aa74-5eb5be9fa04a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106824 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106824 #edb/ref 1061 35855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c850f239-ce6c-5c3e-8ee3-0ed3c4d28237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106825 #edb/ref 1061 35876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec49a1fe-8e0d-5f91-a696-e05afdb8eeab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106826 #edb/ref 1061 35879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "369de3f2-63c7-51db-879c-c7cb6e22ec92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107127 #edb/ref 1064 "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107127 #edb/ref 1065 5850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107127 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f96a7b70-f713-5ac8-afbe-0bc9a8caf7b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107127 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107128 #edb/ref 1064 "When curves are *negatively* oriented, the procedure of finding the outward-pointing unit normal vector is almost the same. \n\nThe only difference is the second step. If $C$ is negatively oriented (i.e., oriented *clockwise*), we must rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\n![](images/76/7641dfd05a69d6ffa2d0619d1de4a8d3bb6ec63b479b929d0f8e52bf910c9448.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107128 #edb/ref 1065 5861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107128 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "997b1386-6f87-5686-9745-a45b89423d14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107128 #edb/ref 1067 "Case of Negatively Oriented Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107129 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107129 #edb/ref 1065 5864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107129 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d4a8dc89-6fbc-58b6-af1b-92bb93b085ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107129 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the General Formula for a Positively Oriented Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428252 #edb/ref 1030 0.25 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428252 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 4 eligible passed source activities = 0.250000; nearest selected fraction = 1/4. Crossing