6445291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78d41512-3eb5-5609-aad8-a61279c74c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445291 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79ef7418-2499-5a5e-9753-d6a82e3d1195" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445292 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7df57c40-4642-538c-b446-11650ad2af53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445293 #edb/ref 1074 "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01d78fae-df26-523a-a169-023a86387db3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186445293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "091b59dd-5e66-5d54-a04a-5e006f7a4412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186106040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "792a52ce-5964-52dd-90b1-8b9d723859c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186445291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a61f17a9-02c9-5d8f-bc89-f080656ef9f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186106043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445298 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186445296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3c4339d-e499-5428-8be7-e53653cc5d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 175